{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently above realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path (largest early-horizon), rather than only tweaking slope.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear decline, the model projects too-flat or even rebounds too early. Enforce stronger negative drift for the first ~10–15 days, then taper.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model often predicts mean-reversion rallies (mid-horizon bumps) that do not materialize; realized path continues lower/sideways. Damp mid-horizon (days ~10–25) upward curvature; cap any rebound magnitude relative to recent volatility.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: errors are largest at short horizons (days 1–10) where the model misses continued selloff; later horizons still biased high but less. Use a declining correction factor with horizon (strongest front-loaded adjustment).",
    "Regime rule: if the 30-day history shows a sharp peak-to-trough drop (large negative momentum), treat it as a bearish regime—reduce forecast levels and suppress mean-reversion signals until momentum stabilizes (e.g., several days of non-declining closes)."
  ]
}
